            <ArticleTitle>Development of a Measure to Assess Parent Perceptions of Barriers to Child Weight Management.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1089/chi.2017.0171</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Families face many barriers related to creating or maintaining a healthy lifestyle, which likely contributes to the prevalence of childhood obesity in the United States; however, no measure currently exists to examine these barriers. This study developed a quantitative measure of parents' perceptions of barriers to family healthy lifestyle.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Parents of children between the ages of 7-17 were recruited using Amazon Mturk (n = 812). Exploratory and confirmatory factor analyses and preliminary convergent validity analyses were conducted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Four factors emerged in the final measure: Parent Disengagement, Cost and Built Environment, Lack of Family Support, and Family Time Constraints, with the final 19-item measure having good initial psychometric properties, including reliability and validity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Future research is needed to examine whether this measure may be used in clinical practice to identify barriers to a healthy family lifestyle, to tailor interventions for families.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
